In a discussion about running a distributed lag model in a Difference-in-Differences (DID) or event study, James inquired about the significance of pre-event coefficients. I explained that these coefficients test for dynamic pre-trends, determining if treated and control units were diverging prior to treatment. If pre-event coefficients are insignificant, it supports the parallel trends assumption, while significant coefficients indicate potential violations. James then asked if he should perform a joint significance test on pre-period coefficients if they seemed biased away from zero. I confirmed this is advisable and detailed two related tests: one for joint significance of all pre-period coefficients and another for identifying systematic patterns. The conversation emphasized the importance of robust tests for asserting parallel trends to defend interpretations of post-treatment dynamics as causal.
